A||||2||fiero56||Alignment and driving||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-29-2000||09:41 PM||||I have taken off all of my front suspension, installed new bushings, and put it back on. It is now wildly out of alignment.<BR>The dealership doing my alignment for me is probably a good 5(city, no highway) miles away. I am not concerned about keeping control of the car, but more about any damage I can do to my fairly new front tires. I know they will get some extra wear, but will I be driving with slicks on when I pull into the parking lot after 5 miles?<BR>Question is: will my tires be ok if I drive with the toe, camber and caster way out of alignment for 5 miles, or will it be ok?||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||08-29-2000||09:52 PM||||Is it scrubbing really bad?<P>Set the camber as close as possible. A simple bubble square. Just get the tilt close.<P>Then eyeball the toe or measure the distance between the tires at the front and back.<P>That will be close enough to drive it to the shop. Don't go 60 MPH.<P>If it looks close, it close enough for that short distance. <P>If you notice any noises, it's not close enough.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||N_Man||08-30-2000||03:45 AM||||If you got the time and energy it is very possible to get your alignment nearly spot on.<P>Basicly you want to set up a perfect rectangle, made of string, around your car.  Make it about hub height.  Using the string as reference measure your wheel at the front and back, that will give you toe in/out measurements.  A level and a tabe measure will give you camber measurements.  And a plumb bob will give you caster.<P>The rectangle setup is probably the most difficult part, and only essencial for toe.  Every thing else is farily straight foward but time consuming.<P>No you probably wont get the best alignment of your life.  but it sure will be good enough to drive on for lots more than 5 miles||||reg||1
